==Latvian==

===Etymology===
A {{borrowing|lv|orv|умъ||mind, intellect|tr=umŭ|nocap=1}} ([[cf]]. {{cog|ru|ум||mind, intellect, wit|tr=um}}), cognate with {{cog|lt|aumuõ||understanding, notion, intellect}}, [[genitive]] {{m|lt|aumeñs}}. This word was borrowed into {{etyl|lv|-}} before the 13th century, while {{etyl|orv|-}} ''у'' was still close to [o] in pronunciation. It conserved its original meaning (“mind,” “understanding”) well into the 19th century; the modern sense was an innovation introduced by [[w:Atis Kronvalds|A. Kronvalds]].<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{lv-IPA|uōma|level}}
{{rfap|lang=lv}}

===Noun===
{{lv-noun|f|4th}}

# [[mood]] {{gloss|[[mental]] or [[emotional]] [[state]]}}
#: {{ux|lv|būt labā '''omā'''|to be in a good '''mood'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|būt priecīgā '''omā'''|to be in a cheerful '''mood'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|viņš šodien ir sliktā '''omā'''|he is in a bad '''mood''' today|inline=1}}

====Declension====
{{lv-decl-noun|om|a|4th}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|lv|omulīgs}}, {{l|lv|omulīgums}}
* {{l|lv|omulība}}

